    <string name="settings_kill_switch">Kill Switch</string>
    <!-- REMEDIATION-DECISIONS §2 wording. What it does, and no more: the block
         is the app's own, so it holds while BirdoVPN's VPN service is running
         (audit 2026-09-29, D-7; the caveat is the corrected §2 one, second-pass
         #5). Since the 2026-09-30 overhaul the app releases its own block when
         reconnecting keeps failing (parity with iOS and Windows), and Android's
         Always-on VPN IS supported (SUPPORTS_ALWAYS_ON=true, pinned by
         ApiLevel36ContractTest), so the system-level block may be named. -->
    <string name="settings_kill_switch_desc">If the tunnel drops unexpectedly, blocks traffic until it reconnects. If reconnecting keeps failing, the app stops blocking. Protection applies while BirdoVPN\'s VPN service is running. For a block Android enforces at all times, turn on Always-on VPN and Block connections without VPN in Android\'s VPN settings.</string>
    <string name="settings_kill_switch_disable_title">Disable kill switch?</string>
    <!-- D-7: this used to end "for a system-guaranteed block, enable Android's
         Always-on VPN with Block connections without VPN", advice for a setting
         the app then disabled. Always-on is supported since 2026-09-30 and the
         Kill Switch row now names it; this dialog keeps the REMEDIATION-DECISIONS
         text until that document is revised. -->
    <string name="settings_kill_switch_disable_msg">If the VPN drops while the kill switch is off, your apps fall back to your normal, unencrypted connection and can expose your real IP address and DNS queries until it reconnects. For the strongest protection, keep it on.</string>
    <string name="settings_kill_switch_disable_confirm">Turn off anyway</string>
